um text-foreground\",\"children\":\"Salary After Tax\"}],[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"className\":\"text-xs text-muted-foreground\",\"children\":\"Take-home pay for every salary level\"}]]}]]}]]}]]}]\n25:[\"$\",\"div\",null,{\"className\":\"mt-12 space-y-10\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"section\",null,{\"className\":\"rounded-xl border border-border/30 bg-card/50 p-6 sm:p-8\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"h2\",null,{\"className\":\"text-2xl font-bold text-foreground mb-4\",\"children\":\"How This Calculator Works\"}],[\"$\",\"div\",null,{\"className\":\"space-y-4\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"p\",\"0\",{\"className\":\"text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed\",\"dangerouslySetInnerHTML\":{\"__html\":\"Look at your pay stub sometime. That number at the bottom — the one that actually hits your bank — is way smaller than the number at the top. This calculator tells you why, line by line. All withholding calculations follow <a href=\\\"https://www.irs.gov/publications/p15t\\\" target=\\\"_blank\\\" rel=\\\"noopener noreferrer\\\">IRS Publication 15-T</a>.\"}}],[\"$\",\"p\",\"1\",{\"className\":\"text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed\",\"dangerouslySetInnerHTML\":{\"__html\":\"Here's what comes out of every paycheck. Federal tax uses progressive brackets from 10% up to 37%, with standard deductions of $16,100 (single) or $32,200 (married). Then <a href=\\\"/glossary\\\">FICA</a>: 6.2% for Social Security on income up to $184,500, and 1.45% for Medicare on everything. Make over $200,000? Add another 0.9% Medicare surtax on the amount above that.\"}}],[\"$\",\"p\",\"2\",{\"className\":\"text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed\",\"dangerouslySetInnerHTML\":{\"__html\":\"My buddy in Chicago and I compared stubs once. Same salary, same filing status. He walked away with about $3,800 less for the year because Illinois takes 4.95% and my state takes nothing. That's when it hit me — state tax is not a small factor. We cover five states here: Illinois at 4.95% flat, Texas at 0%, Florida at 0%, California at 1%–13.3% progressive, and New York at 4%–10.9% plus a potential NYC tax.\"}}],[\"$\",\"p\",\"3\",{\"className\":\"text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed\",\"dangerouslySetInnerHTML\":{\"__html\":\"A couple things that help soften the blow:<br/>- <a href=\\\"/401k-retirement-calculator\\\">401(k) contributions</a> reduce taxable income at both federal and state level<br/>- HSA contributions do the same<br/>- These pre-tax deductions are basically a discount on your tax bill\"}}],[\"$\",\"p\",\"4\",{\"className\":\"text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed\",\"dangerouslySetInnerHTML\":{\"__html\":\"Bottom line — you'll see your net pay, effective tax rate, and marginal rate. Most people think their effective rate is higher than it actually is. Go ahead, see for yourself.\"}}]]}]]}],[\"$\",\"section\",null,{\"className\":\"rounded-xl border border-emerald-500/20 bg-emerald-500/5 p-6 sm:p-8\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"h2\",null,{\"className\":\"text-2xl font-bold text-foreground mb-4\",\"children\":\"How It's Calculated\"}],[\"$\",\"div\",null,{\"className\":\"space-y-5\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"div\",null,{\"children\":[[\"$\",\"h3\",null,{\"className\":\"text-sm font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider text-emerald-400 mb-2\",\"children\":\"Formula\"}],[\"$\",\"div\",null,{\"className\":\"rounded-lg bg-background/50 border border-border/30 p-4 font-mono text-sm text-foreground\",\"children\":\"Net Pay = Gross Income − (Federal Tax + State Tax + FICA + Pre-tax Deductions)\"}]]}],[\"$\",\"div\",null,{\"children\":[[\"$\",\"h3\",null,{\"className\":\"text-sm font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider text-emerald-400 mb-2\",\"children\":\"Step-by-Step\"}],[\"$\",\"ol\",null,{\"className\":\"space-y-2\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"li\",\"0\",{\"className\":\"flex gap-3 text-sm text-muted-foreground\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"className\":\"flex h-6 w-6 shrink-0 items-center justify-center rounded-full bg-emerald-500/20 text-xs font-bold text-emerald-400\",\"children\":1}],[\"$\"